\cf0 [Chapter 4]\
\
Two common patterns:\
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 Model loaded into each server instance
\f1\b0  \'97 each replica pulls the model from the distributed DB on startup (or on first request), loads it into memory, and runs inference locally. The "trained ML model" box in the diagram is then just a logical depiction of what's happening inside each instance, not a separate service. This is the more common pattern for smaller models. 
\f3\i\fs24 (Model lives on the same servicing layer who accepts incoming users request directly)
\f1\i0\fs26 \

\f0\b Separate inference service
\f1\b0  \'97 the model server instances are truly just a routing/preprocessing layer, and they call out to a dedicated GPU inference cluster (think 
\f0\b Triton Inference Server, TorchServe, or vLLM
\f1\b0 ). The model server is CPU-bound (parsing requests, feature assembly, postprocessing), and the inference backend is GPU-bound. This matches your intuition exactly. 
\f3\i\fs24 (Model prediction and user req processing sits in separate layer/clusters)
\f1\i0\fs26 \
\
