\cf0 2- What\'92s the concrete format of the binary files in downloaded dataset\
Your files are in 
\f0\b IDX format
\f1\b0  \'97 a simple binary format invented for MNIST. Let me decode it:\
\pard\pardeftab560\sa40\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 [Images file]
\f1\b0  (train-images.idx3-ubyte):
\fs32 \
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0

\fs26 \cf0 File size: 47,040,016 bytes\
\
HEADER (16 bytes):\
  bytes 0-3:   magic number  = 2051    (means "this is images")\
  bytes 4-7:   num images    = 60,000\
  bytes 8-11:  num rows      = 28\
  bytes 12-15: num cols      = 28\
\
DATA (after header):\
  60,000 \'d7 28 \'d7 28 = 47,040,000 bytes\
  47,040,000 + 16 header = 47,040,016 
\f4 \uc0\u10003 
\f1  matches your file!\
\
Each byte = one pixel\
  value 0   = black\
  value 255 = white\
  values in between = grey shades\
\
That\'92s why parquet can be used as format to house unstructured dataset, images / videos as such\
\
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 [Labels file]
\f1\b0  (train-labels.idx1-ubyte):\
File size: 60,008 bytes\
\
HEADER (8 bytes):\
  bytes 0-3:  magic number = 2049  (means "this is labels")\
  bytes 4-7:  num labels   = 60,000\
\
DATA:\
  60,000 \'d7 1 byte = 60,000 bytes\
  60,000 + 8 header = 60,008 
\f4 \uc0\u10003 
\f1  matches your file!\
\
Each byte = one label\
  value 0 = T-shirt/top\
  value 1 = Trouser\
  value 2 = Pullover\
  ...\
  value 9 = Ankle boot\
\

