\f0\b \cf0 Ray Data - Shuffling
\f1\b0 \
```\
ds = ray.data.read_parquet(\'91parquet_file_path\'92)\
ds\
  .random_shuffle()  -> lazy shuffle, will start the shuffling at first time of iterating batches\
  .materialize()  -> not lazy, materialize it into arrow table into ray\'92s objstore shared mem here right away.\
```\
\
\
Ray\'92s shuffling process: say 2 workers\
1- 2 workers each read their assigned shards 
\f4 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  write Arrow tables to ObjStore, if random_shuffle() is called, when reading into pyarrow tables into objstore shared memory, each worker adds a random float column to each row before writing to ObjStore\
Reader Worker 0:  shard 0,1 
\f4 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  ObjStore partition A\
Reader Worker 1:  shard 2,3 
\f4 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  ObjStore partition B\
2- Workers do global Sort-Merge\
Ray spawns REDUCER tasks ( can be the same workers that does the read)\
Do range-based distributed sort\
	Partition A and B\'92s rows with range 0-0.5 -> reducer0\
	Partition A and B\'92s Range 0-0.5 -> reducer1\
  - Do partial sort within each partition \
  - merge-sort across partitions\
  - write final shuffled partitions back to ObjStore\
Final ObjStore state: \
	output_partition_0: all rows with key [0.0 
\f4 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  0.5), sorted by key \
	output_partition_1: all rows with key [0.5 
\f4 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  1.0], sorted by key\
\
Since pyarrow table are immutable, sorting always needs additional alloc of mem, so RAM is usually double of the to-be-sorted dataset\
\
\

