\f0\b\fs32 \cf0 How Engines Join Parquet Tables\
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs26 \cf0 When you trigger a join between two Parquet datasets, the compute engine typically uses one of three physical strategies:\
\
\pard\pardeftab560\sa40\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs32 \cf0 1. Broadcast Hash Join (Small + Large) [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://medium.com/@amarkrgupta96/join-strategies-in-apache-spark-a-hands-on-approach-d0696fc0a6c9"}}{\fldrslt \ul 1}}]\
\pard\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0
\f6\b0\fs18 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 How it works
\f1\b0 : The engine sends the entire small table to every worker node in the cluster.\
\ls3\ilvl0
\f6\fs18 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 Optimization
\f1\b0 : This avoids "shuffling" the large table across the network.\
\ls3\ilvl0
\f6\fs18 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 Best for
\f1\b0 : Joining a small dimension table (e.g., user_metadata) with a massive fact table (e.g., user_logs). [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://medium.com/@ykothari99/spark-joins-broadcast-hash-join-spark-joins-series-ec14ae8bb810#:~:text=2.%20How%20Broadcast%20Hash%20Join%20Works%202.1,Dataset/table%202.4%20Join%20Operation%202.5%20Result%20Partitioning"}}{\fldrslt \ul 1}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://medium.com/@mjprub/optimizing-joins-in-apache-spark-the-power-of-broadcast-joins-af920476bbb4#:~:text=Instead%20of%20shuffling%20both%20tables%20across%20the,with%20its%20partition%20of%20the%20large%20table."}}{\fldrslt \ul 2}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://medium.com/art-of-data-engineering/how-spark-performs-joins-a-quick-look-into-small-and-large-table-joins-68ebdc4f5b29#:~:text=When%20a%20table%20is%20small%20enough%20to,all%20worker%20nodes%2C%20avoiding%20expensive%20all%2Dto%2Dall%20communication."}}{\fldrslt \ul 3}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://medium.com/@mohammadshoaib_74869/mastering-apache-spark-joins-3fd338c84d09#:~:text=Always%20Ask%20First:%20Can%20I%20Broadcast?%20Your,Join%20is%20almost%20certainly%20the%20correct%20choice."}}{\fldrslt \ul 4}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.canadiandataguy.com/p/spark-join-strategies-explained-broadcast"}}{\fldrslt \ul 5}}]\
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab560\sa40\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs32 \cf0 2. Sort-Merge Join (Large + Large) [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.unraveldata.com/resources/databricks-slow-joins-join-type-optimization/#:~:text=Sort%2Dmerge%20joins%20serve%20as%20Databricks'%20default%20for,for%20tables%20over%201GB%20on%20both%20sides."}}{\fldrslt \ul 1}}]\
\pard\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0
\f6\b0\fs18 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 How it works
\f1\b0 : Both datasets are sorted by the join key and then merged.\
\ls4\ilvl0
\f6\fs18 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 Optimization
\f1\b0 : If the Parquet files are already sorted on disk by the join key, the engine can skip the expensive sorting phase.\
\ls4\ilvl0
\f6\fs18 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 Best for
\f1\b0 : Joining two massive datasets that don't fit in memory. [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://medium.com/@kerrache.massipssa/apache-spark-join-strategies-in-depth-171bf7fef4b0#:~:text=Sort%20Merge%20Join%20Shuffle:%20The%20data%20from,across%20partitions%20to%20execute%20the%20join%20operation."}}{\fldrslt \ul 1}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/apache-spark-join-strategies-dishant-prajapati-ncu4f#:~:text=Merging%20:%20On%20each%20worker%20node%2C%20the,from%20both%20datasets%20and%20joining%20matching%20records."}}{\fldrslt \ul 2}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/mastering-join-strategies-apache-spark-field-guide-data-deepak-kumar-kcwhc"}}{\fldrslt \ul 3}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://gelovolro.medium.com/what-are-merge-join-hash-join-and-nested-loop-example-in-postgresql-29123ca18fd1#:~:text=Merge%20join%2C%20this%20is%20a%20method%20used%2C,JOIN%20)%20operation%2C%20which%20may%20increase%20costs."}}{\fldrslt \ul 4}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.canadiandataguy.com/p/spark-join-strategies-explained-sort#:~:text=Sorting%20is%20an%20expensive%20step%20(%20O(n,key%20with%20the%20same%20number%20of%20partitions)."}}{\fldrslt \ul 5}}]\
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab560\sa40\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs32 \cf0 3. Shuffle Hash Join\
\pard\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0
\f6\b0\fs18 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 How it works
\f1\b0 : Both tables are partitioned based on the hash of the join key so that matching rows end up on the same worker node.\
\ls5\ilvl0
\f6\fs18 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 Best for
\f1\b0 : Large tables where keys are distributed fairly evenly. [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://celerdata.com/glossary/sql-joins#:~:text=Both%20tables%20are%20partitioned%20(or%20re%2Dpartitioned)%20by,a%20hash%20function%20on%20the%20join%20key."}}{\fldrslt \ul 1}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://medium.com/@mahajansd/join-strategies-in-spark-b26d03d473dd#:~:text=2.%20Shuffle%20Hash%20Join%20Data%20is%20shuffled,one%20side%20and%20probes%20with%20the%20other."}}{\fldrslt \ul 2}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/apache-spark-join-strategies-dishant-prajapati-ncu4f#:~:text=For%20two%20datasets%20that%20need%20to%20be,records%20are%20joined%20locally%20on%20each%20node."}}{\fldrslt \ul 3}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://medium.com/@deepa.account/spark-concepts-and-questions-aaea515d25ef#:~:text=3.%20Shuffle%20Hash%20Join:%20Used%20for%20joining,when%20the%20join%20keys%20are%20evenly%20distributed."}}{\fldrslt \ul 4}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://blog.nashtechglobal.com/advanced-spark-sql-joins-an-optimization-technique/#:~:text=When%20the%20table%20is%20relatively%20large%2C%20the,shuffle%20Hash%20Join%20is%20the%20right%20choice."}}{\fldrslt \ul 5}}]\
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
