\
model = torch.nn.Sequential(torch.nn.Linear(20, 64), torch.nn.ReLU(), torch.nn.Linear(64, 2))\
	
\f0\b Memory trick:
\f1\b0  nn.Linear(in, out) 
\f4 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  weight is (out, in), always reversed.\
output = input @ weight.T + bias (64,) = (20,) @ (20, 64) + (64,)   weight matrix
\f5 \'d7\'dc\'ca\'c7\'d7\'aa\'d6\'c3\'be\'d8\'d5\'f3\'c8\'bb\'ba\'f3\'d4\'d9\'cf\'e0\'b3\'cb
\f1  
\f5 \'d2\'f2\'ce\'aa
\f1 input is alwasy vector 1 x 20 (with 20 as dimension)\
\
model.state_dict : \
k:0.weight:v-type:<class 'torch.Tensor'>  a 64 x 20 tensor (64,20)\
k:0.bias:v-type:<class 'torch.Tensor'> a 1x64 tensor (64,)\
k:2.weight:v-type:<class 'torch.Tensor'> a 2 x 64 tensor (2,64)\
k:2.bias:v-type:<class 'torch.Tensor'>  a 1x2 tensor (2,)\
\
Forward passing flow:\uc0\u8232 input: (batch_size, 20) \

\f4 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  Layer 1 out: (batch_size, 64) \

\f4 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  ReLU: (batch_size, 64) # no shape change \

\f4 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  Layer 2 out: (batch_size, 2)\
\
If batch_size =  1  it starts with a 1x20 vector into a 1 x 2 output\
So say input is a 1x20 vector representing a word , then raw output is a 1x2 (raw output from nn.Linear are called 
\f0\b logits
\f1\b0 , they aren't probabilities yet), they look like:\
raw output: [-1.2, 3.4]   
\f4 \uc0\u8592 
\f1  logits, can be any value\
After softmax it will tell us, for a binary label which label it most likely should fall into \
-> softmax output [0.1, 0.9]\
