\f0\b \cf0 Why TPUs are faster for training
\f1\b0 \
Three reasons:\

\f0\b 1. BF16 is native
\f1\b0  TPUs were designed around BF16 (brain float 16) \'97 the format Google invented for ML. Operations in BF16 on a TPU are extremely fast. GPUs support BF16 too but it's less native.\

\f0\b 2. The MXU never waits
\f1\b0  The Matrix Multiply Unit (MXU) on a TPU is designed so data flows continuously \'97 it's always multiplying something. 
\f0\b GPUs have more overhead scheduling work across CUDA cores.
\f1\b0 \

\f0\b 3. ICI \'97 the interconnect
\f1\b0  This is the big one for large model training. TPU pods connect chips via ICI, a custom high-bandwidth low-latency mesh network built into the hardware. A TPU v4 pod has 4096 chips all connected with ~
\f0\b 1.1 Pb/s 
\f1\b0 of total bisection bandwidth. 
\f0\b This makes all-reduce (the core operation in distributed training) dramatically faster than even NVLink between GPUs.
\f1\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0
\cf0 GPU + CUDA \
  Like sys call, u control thread creation destroy, mem alloc/dealloc every step of the entire program\
TPU + XLA \
   compiles 
\f3\i your
\f1\i0  specific computation fresh each time, nothing below \'91computation graph\'92 level, like writing SQL \'97 you declare 
\f3\i what
\f1\i0  you want, the query planner figures out 
\f3\i how. (
\f0\i0\b jit \'97 Just-In-Time compilation
\f1\b0  Compiles your Python function to XLA (Google's accelerator compiler). First call is slow (compilation), every subsequent call is fast. This is how JAX gets GPU performance without writing CUDA.  
\f0\b jit is kind of like query planner
\f1\b0 )\
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
